   SHAPE — glide PAST the spot, then settle back. Two moves, not three: the word travels in
   from its offset and carries a little beyond its resting place, then eases back onto it.
   Pure translation — nothing changes size at any point.

   The overshoot is deliberately part of the ARRIVAL rather than something that happens after
   it. An earlier version landed on the spot first and only then hopped away and back, which
   is three separate moves and reads as the word being nudged after it has already settled.
   Carrying through and easing back is one continuous gesture.

   The motion is HORIZONTAL only — there is no vertical variant. Two keyframe sets cover every
   case: crazy2InRight travels left-to-right (starting off to the left) and crazy2InLeft travels
   right-to-left. Which one a word gets is decided per WORD, not per line, in the renderer:
   a line holding one word follows its line's direction (lines 1 and 3 left-to-right, line 2
   right-to-left), and a line holding two words sends them in OPPOSITE directions so they close
   on their places from either side.

   Note the overshoot sign is always OPPOSITE the starting offset — the word keeps going the
   way it was already travelling, then eases back.

   Each interval carries its OWN animation-timing-function:
     • glide  — cubic-bezier(0.18, 0.72, 0.35, 1): quick off the mark, decelerating the whole
                way, coming to a standstill at the far point of the overshoot;
     • return — cubic-bezier(0.4, 0.02, 0.3, 1): barely moves as it leaves the overshoot and
                eases the whole way into place. Both ends gentle is what keeps this from
                reading as a jolt — an earlier hard ease-in here slammed the word onto the
                spot and stopped it dead.
   The 'linear' in the shorthand below is only a formality — every interval overrides it.

   DISTANCE. The approach is 1.25em for every word, now that all of them travel on the same
   axis — well over three times the reference's 20-22px. Coming from that far out gives the eye
   a real path to follow rather than the word seeming to materialise near its place. The
   overshoot is 0.15em / 0.16em past the spot — small on purpose. It only has to read as
   momentum carrying through; any further and the word visibly travels somewhere it does not
   belong before correcting itself, which looks like a mistake rather than a bounce.

   DURATION. 0.90s: the glide owns the first 68% (0.61s) and the settle back takes the
   remaining 0.29s. The turnaround sits earlier than it used to precisely so the return has
   more room — a shorter distance covered over a longer time is what makes it read smoothly. Note the AUTHORED length is only half the story — see
   CRAZY2_MIN_ENTRANCE_SCALE in Crazy2Text.js, which stops the shared --entrance-scale squeeze
   compressing all of this down to the last word's duration.

   To retune: 68% moves the turnaround (earlier = a longer, gentler return), 1.25em is how far
   it travels in from, and 0.16em is how far past the spot it runs. Letter-by-letter overrides only the DURATION (see the .char-span rule
   below the classes); the shape and distances are shared by both modes.

   It all rides on ONE keyframe set rather than a second stacked animation because
   .capture-freeze seeks exactly one --cap-delay per element, so two animations on the same
   span could not be seeked independently for the export (the same constraint that gave
   Punch's exit its own wrapper). */
@keyframes crazy2InRight {
  0%   { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-1.25em);
         animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.18, 0.72, 0.35, 1); }
  68%  { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0.16em);
         animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0.02, 0.3, 1); }
  100% { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); }
}
@keyframes crazy2InLeft {
  0%   { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(1.25em);
         animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.18, 0.72, 0.35, 1); }
  68%  { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(-0.16em);
         animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0.02, 0.3, 1); }
  100% { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); }
}
.anim-crazy2-right .word-span,
.anim-crazy2-right .char-span {
  opacity: 0;
  /* Promoted to its own layer: these spans carry a blurred text-shadow, and repainting that
     glow every frame while the span slides is what made the motion stutter. */
  will-change: transform, opacity;
  animation: crazy2InRight calc(0.90s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) linear forwards;
}
.anim-crazy2-left .word-span,
.anim-crazy2-left .char-span {
  opacity: 0;
  /* Promoted to its own layer: these spans carry a blurred text-shadow, and repainting that
     glow every frame while the span slides is what made the motion stutter. */
  will-change: transform, opacity;
  animation: crazy2InLeft calc(0.90s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) linear forwards;
}

/* Letter by letter runs a little quicker per CHARACTER than word-by-word does. The stagger
   already spreads a word out across the run — a long word finishes a whole letter-step after
   its first character starts — so at the full word duration the tail of a long word arrived
   noticeably late. Shortening only the per-character animation tightens that up without
   touching the word-by-word feel. Keep in sync with CRAZY2_CHAR_SEC in Crazy2Text.js, which
   is what the exporter tiles against. */
.anim-crazy2-right .char-span,
.anim-crazy2-left .char-span {
  animation-duration: calc(0.78s * var(--entrance-scale, 1));
}

/* Solo — each word fades in at its own spoken time. Opacity-only (no transform in the
   keyframe) because Solo words carry their own translate/scale/rotate inline transform,
   which an animated transform would clobber. */
@keyframes soloFadeIn {
  from { opacity: 0; }
  to   { opacity: 1; }
}
.solo-word {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: soloFadeIn calc(0.25s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease forwards;
}

/* 3. Slide From Left */
@keyframes captionSlideFromLeft {
  0% { transform: translateX(-50px); opacity: 0; }
  100% { transform: translateX(0); opacity: 1; }
}
.anim-slide-left .word-span {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionSlideFromLeft calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}

/* 3b. Slide From Right — mirror of Slide From Left */
@keyframes captionSlideFromRight {
  0% { transform: translateX(50px); opacity: 0; }
  100% { transform: translateX(0); opacity: 1; }
}
.anim-slide-right .word-span {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionSlideFromRight calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}

/* 4. Bounce Drop */
@keyframes captionBounceDrop {
  0% { transform: translateY(-50px); opacity: 0; }
  40% { transform: translateY(0); opacity: 1; }
  60% { transform: translateY(-15px); }
  80% { transform: translateY(0); }
  90% { transform: translateY(-5px); }
  100% { transform: translateY(0); opacity: 1; }
}
.anim-bounce-drop .word-span {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionBounceDrop calc(0.6s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}

/* 5. Typewriter (Character based) */
@keyframes captionTypeWriter {
  from { opacity: 0; }
  to { opacity: 1; }
}
.anim-typewriter .char-span {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionTypeWriter calc(0.05s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) linear forwards;
}

/* 6. Flicker/Glitch */
@keyframes captionFlicker {
  0%, 19%, 21%, 23%, 25%, 54%, 56%, 100% { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); }
  20%, 24%, 55% { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.95); }
}
.anim-flicker .word-span {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionPopIn calc(0.2s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) forwards, captionFlicker calc(0.5s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-in-out forwards calc(0.2s * var(--entrance-scale, 1));
}

/* 7. Character Snap/Scale */
@keyframes captionCharSnap {
  0% { transform: scale(2) rotate(15deg); opacity: 0; }
  100% { transform: scale(1) rotate(0deg); opacity: 1; }
}
.anim-char-snap .char-span {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionCharSnap calc(0.3s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.46, 0.45, 0.94) forwards;
}

/* 8. Rotate In 3D */
@keyframes captionRotateIn3D {
  0% { transform: perspective(400px) rotateX(90deg); opacity: 0; }
  100% { transform: perspective(400px) rotateX(0deg); opacity: 1; }
}
.anim-rotate-3d .word-span {
  opacity: 0;
  transform-origin: bottom;
  animation: captionRotateIn3D calc(0.5s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}

/* 9. Blur Reveal — composes with --word-glow for the same reason captionSlamIn does: it animates
   the filter property, so a bare blur(0) end state would erase a template's own glow for good. */
@keyframes captionBlurReveal {
  0% { filter: blur(10px) var(--word-glow, ); opacity: 0; transform: scale(1.1); }
  100% { filter: var(--word-glow, blur(0px)); opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); }
}
.anim-blur .word-span {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionBlurReveal calc(0.5s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}

/* 10. Word Spring (Hormozi style) */
@keyframes captionSpringWord {
  0% { transform: scale(0.5); opacity: 0; color: #fff; }
  50% { transform: scale(1.2); opacity: 1; color: #fff; }
  100% { transform: scale(1); opacity: 1; color: #fff; }
}
.anim-spring .word-span {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionSpringWord calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) cubic-bezier(0.175, 0.885, 0.32, 1.275) forwards;
}
.anim-spring.playing .word-span.past {
  color: #94a3b8;
  transform: scale(0.9);
  transition: all 0.3s ease;
}

/* Common helper classes */
.word-span {
  display: inline-block;
  white-space: pre;
}
.char-span {
  display: inline-block;
  white-space: pre;
}

/* ─── Block-level transitions for themes that can't use per-word .word-span ───
 * Shine, Fusion, Triple, Pill, Sliding-Highlight, Popup-Chat, Notification themes
 * render their text inside a single visual element (background-clip:text gradient,
 * pill badge, highlight box, etc.) where wrapping individual words in spans would
 * break the rendering. For these themes the transition CSS class lands on a
 * .block-anim-target wrapper, and these rules animate the whole wrapper instead
 * of per-word. Same animations, same feel — just applied at block granularity.
 * Note: anim-blur is intentionally omitted because filter:blur breaks
 * background-clip:text gradient rendering in WebKit.
 */
.anim-pop > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionPopIn calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) cubic-bezier(0.175, 0.885, 0.32, 1.275) forwards;
}
.anim-slide-up > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionSlideUpFade calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}
.anim-slide-down-fade > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionSlideDownFade calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}
.anim-slide-left > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionSlideFromLeft calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}
.anim-slide-right > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionSlideFromRight calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}
.anim-bounce-drop > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionBounceDrop calc(0.6s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}
.anim-typewriter > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionTypeWriter calc(0.3s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) linear forwards;
}
.anim-flicker > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionPopIn calc(0.2s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) forwards, captionFlicker calc(0.5s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-in-out forwards calc(0.2s * var(--entrance-scale, 1));
}
.anim-char-snap > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionCharSnap calc(0.3s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.46, 0.45, 0.94) forwards;
}
.anim-rotate-3d > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  transform-origin: bottom;
  animation: captionRotateIn3D calc(0.5s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}
.anim-blur > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  /* Use scale-only fade instead of blur — filter:blur breaks background-clip:text */
  animation: captionPopIn calc(0.5s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) ease-out forwards;
}
.anim-spring > .block-anim-target {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: captionSpringWord calc(0.4s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) cubic-bezier(0.175, 0.885, 0.32, 1.275) forwards;
}

/* ─── Notification-only entrance animations ───
 * Applied directly to .notif-wrapper. The wrapper's own default animation is
 * removed (replaced) by these so the per-block transition controls the entrance.
 */
@keyframes notifSlideDown {
  0%   { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-60px) scale(0.92); }
  60%  { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(4px) scale(1); }
  100% { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0) scale(1); }
}
.anim-notif-slide-down.notif-wrapper {
  opacity: 0;
  transform: translateY(-60px) scale(0.92);
  animation: notifSlideDown calc(0.7s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1) forwards;
  animation-delay: 0.05s;
}

@keyframes notifPop {
  0%   { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0); }
  70%  { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1.06); }
  100% { opacity: 1; transform: scale(1); }
}
.anim-notif-pop.notif-wrapper {
  opacity: 0;
  transform: scale(0);
  transform-origin: center;
  animation: notifPop calc(0.45s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1) forwards;
  animation-delay: 0.05s;
}

@keyframes notifSlideLeft {
  0%   { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(-120%); }
  100% { opacity: 1; transform: translateX(0); }
}
.anim-notif-slide-left.notif-wrapper {
  opacity: 0;
  transform: translateX(-120%);
  animation: notifSlideLeft calc(0.5s * var(--entrance-scale, 1)) cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) forwards;
  animation-delay: 0.05s;
}

.cook-in-fade, .cook-in-pop, .cook-in-up, .cook-in-down,
.cook-in-left, .cook-in-right {
  animation-name: cookHoleIn;
  animation-duration: 0.42s;
  animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1);
  animation-fill-mode: both;
}
@keyframes cookHoleIn { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }

